<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The RELEASE lane's entitlements: three, and the absence of a fourth is the
  point of the file.

  Chromium's renderers JIT, so `allow-jit` and `allow-unsigned-executable-memory`
  are not optional for an Electron app under a hardened runtime; without them
  the app dies at first paint. `automation.apple-events` is what lets the
  daemon drive Messages.app, which is the entire product.

  What is deliberately NOT here is `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation`.
  Library validation is the check that refuses to load a Mach-O signed by a
  different team than the loading process, and turning it off is the single
  most load-bearing security property this bundle has: it is what stops a
  dylib dropped beside the app from being loaded into a process that holds
  Full Disk Access and Automation rights over the user's messages. Under a
  Developer ID signature the app and `better-sqlite3` share one Team ID, so
  validation passes and the entitlement is not needed. It IS needed in the
  ad-hoc lane, where there is no team at all, which is why that lane has a
  second file rather than this one growing a fourth key.

  electron-builder's own default template includes `disable-library-validation`
  and omits `automation.apple-events`. Both deltas are why `electron-builder.yml`
  names this file explicitly and why Sc 6 row 6 deep-equals the sorted key set:
  a default that happens to work is a default that ships the wrong thing the
  day it stops happening to work.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
